/target
*.o
*.ll
# Compiled Burxt outputs are bare, extensionless executables. `burxt build -o <path>`
# is the way to keep them out of the tree; without it they land in the working
# directory, where they are easy to commit by accident — two slipped into v0.0.40 that
# way, and by v0.0.70 the root held twenty-six. Ignore anything with no extension at the
# repository root, then re-admit the files that genuinely belong there: a rule that
# holds for programs nobody has written yet, rather than a list to be remembered.
/*
!/*.*
!/.gitignore
!/.gitattributes
!/LICENSE-APACHE
!/LICENSE-MIT
!/assets/
!/editors/
!/examples/
!/spec/
!/src/
!/tests/
# Three directories the whitelist forgot. `lib/` is the standard library, `docs/` holds the
# guide AND the entire milestone log, and `scripts/` builds the release — all of them read by
# tests, none of them committed, from v0.0.31 until v0.0.105 found it. A whitelist is safer
# than a blacklist for stray binaries and strictly more dangerous for new directories: the
# failure is silent, and `git status` stays clean while the work is not saved.
!/docs/
!/lib/
!/scripts/

# The DOT-directories, admitted explicitly. All four were already tracked — but only by accident:
# `!/*.*` was written to re-admit root FILES like `README.md`, and `.github` happens to satisfy
# `*.*` because of its leading dot.
#
# What that accident risks, stated precisely rather than dramatically: already-tracked files stay
# tracked whatever this file says, so nothing would vanish. The danger is a NEW file. Tighten
# `!/*.*` to something more careful — `!/*.md`, say, which is a tempting improvement — and the next
# workflow, devcontainer feature or vscode setting added here is silently unversioned while
# `git status` stays clean. Verified by doing it: a new `.github/workflows/` file matched `/*` and
# disappeared.
#
# That is the shape, if not the scale, of what hid docs/, lib/ and scripts/ from v0.0.31 to
# v0.0.105. Naming these four means the whitelist says what it means rather than working by luck.
!/.cargo/
!/.devcontainer/
!/.github/
!/.vscode/

# release tarballs: built by scripts/release.sh, published as GitHub release assets,
# never committed — a binary in a repository is a binary nobody can reproduce
/dist/

# editor tooling scratch
node_modules/
*.vsix

# transient delivery artifacts, and the marker Windows attaches to downloads
*.zip
*.tar.gz
*:Zone.Identifier
__pycache__/
